`symfony-security` holds the security contracts several packages of the suite share, so that each one says only what is its own: what its secrets look like, what its security facts are called. It is a plain library — no bundle to register, no configuration: `symfony-user` and `symfony-api` depend on it, an application uses it the same way.

- **Secret redaction in logs**: `Log\AbstractSecretRedactionProcessor` walks every Monolog record — message, context, extra, array keys, carried exceptions with their previous ones, objects as the formatter reads them — and hands each string to the subclass's `mask()`.
- **Security events**: `Event\AbstractSecurityEvent`, one shape for every security fact (type, outcome, cause, user id, method, firewall, IP, user agent, request id, UTC time, extra); `EventSubscriber\AbstractSecurityEventLogSubscriber` writes them to a channel of their own; `Helper\RequestIdHelper` gives one request id per request to every package.
